; Pako Setup's English text: Tauri's own English file (tauri-cli 2.12) with plainer wording for
; closing Pako, for replacing an older version (installer/installer.nsi then only removes the old
; files, see "[Pako]" there) and for the uninstaller's "delete data" box. Tauri needs every
; string it uses here: after a tauri-cli update, compare with its English.nsh and add any new one.
;
; [Pako] Each line is `!insertmacro PakoEn <name> "<text>"` (installer/ui.nsh): it makes both Tauri's
; LangString <name> (read as $(name) by Tauri's own code) and the define PakoEn_<name>, which
; Setup's window reads through PakoT, so that its words can switch to French and back while it is
; open (a LangString always gives the language Setup started in). French.nsh has the same names.
!insertmacro PakoEn addOrReinstall "Add/Reinstall components"
!insertmacro PakoEn alreadyInstalled "Already Installed"
!insertmacro PakoEn alreadyInstalledLong "${PRODUCTNAME} ${VERSION} is already installed. Select the operation you want to perform and click Next to continue."
!insertmacro PakoEn appRunning "{{product_name}} is open. Close it first, then try again."
!insertmacro PakoEn appRunningOkKill "{{product_name}} is open.$\nClick OK to close it and continue."
!insertmacro PakoEn chooseMaintenanceOption "Choose the maintenance option to perform."
!insertmacro PakoEn chooseHowToInstall "Choose how you want to install ${PRODUCTNAME}."
!insertmacro PakoEn createDesktop "Create desktop shortcut"
!insertmacro PakoEn dontUninstall "Do not uninstall"
!insertmacro PakoEn dontUninstallDowngrade "Do not uninstall (Downgrading without uninstall is disabled for this installer)"
!insertmacro PakoEn failedToKillApp "Couldn't close {{product_name}}. Close it first, then try again."
; (tauri-cli 2.12.5's utils.nsh says it when Windows doesn't let Setup close the app.)
!insertmacro PakoEn noPermissionToCloseApp "{{product_name}} can't be closed from here. Close it yourself, then try again."
!insertmacro PakoEn installingWebview2 "Installing WebView2..."
!insertmacro PakoEn newerVersionInstalled "A newer version of ${PRODUCTNAME} is already installed! It is not recommended that you install an older version. If you really want to install this older version, it's better to uninstall the current version first. Select the operation you want to perform and click Next to continue."
!insertmacro PakoEn older "older"
!insertmacro PakoEn olderOrUnknownVersionInstalled "An $R4 version of ${PRODUCTNAME} is installed. Pako Setup replaces it: your settings, lists, shortcuts and taskbar pin stay. Click Next to continue."
!insertmacro PakoEn silentDowngrades "Downgrades are disabled for this installer, can't proceed with the silent installer, please use the graphical interface installer instead.$\n"
!insertmacro PakoEn unableToUninstall "Unable to uninstall!"
!insertmacro PakoEn uninstallApp "Uninstall ${PRODUCTNAME}"
!insertmacro PakoEn uninstallBeforeInstalling "Replace the installed version (recommended)"
!insertmacro PakoEn unknown "unknown"
!insertmacro PakoEn webview2AbortError "Failed to install WebView2! The app can't run without it. Try restarting the installer."
!insertmacro PakoEn webview2DownloadError "Error: Downloading WebView2 Failed - $0"
!insertmacro PakoEn webview2DownloadSuccess "WebView2 bootstrapper downloaded successfully"
!insertmacro PakoEn webview2Downloading "Downloading WebView2 bootstrapper..."
!insertmacro PakoEn webview2InstallError "Error: Installing WebView2 failed with exit code $1"
!insertmacro PakoEn webview2InstallSuccess "WebView2 installed successfully"
!insertmacro PakoEn deleteAppData "Also delete Pako's settings and lists"
; [Pako] A silent or passive install's folder check (installer/hooks.nsh: PakoCheckDir).
!insertmacro PakoEn pakoDirNotWritable "Pako Setup installs Pako for you only, without admin rights, so it needs a folder you can change. Windows doesn't let you change $INSTDIR (folders like Program Files need admin rights).$\n$\nPick another folder, for example $LOCALAPPDATA\Programs\Pako."
!insertmacro PakoEn pakoDirNotWritableSilent "Pako Setup installs Pako for you only, without admin rights, so it needs a folder you can change. Windows doesn't let you change $INSTDIR (folders like Program Files need admin rights). Pick another folder with /D=, for example /D=$LOCALAPPDATA\Programs\Pako$\n"
; [Pako] Pako Setup's own window (installer/ui.nsh). Plain words, the window's exact labels.
